<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munich Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Munich Pact</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Agreement was concluded on 29–30 September 1938 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> taking part. It required Czechoslovakia to cede the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> to Nazi Germany, which Hitler had threatened to seize by force. Hailed by Neville Chamberlain as 'peace for our time', it became the symbol of appeasement; Germany occupied the rest of the Czech lands in March 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munich-agreement.jpg" alt="Munich Agreement" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Munich_Agreement_Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R69173.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was created in 1918 after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. Its population included more than three million Germans, 22.95 percent of the total, who lived mostly in the border regions that came to be called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a>. They had not been consulted on becoming citizens, and although the constitution promised equality they remained underrepresented in the government and the army. By 1936 60 percent of the unemployed in Czechoslovakia were Germans.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> wished to avoid war. The French government followed the British government of Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain, who considered the Sudeten German grievances justified. Both advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> to give way to German demands. President Beneš resisted and ordered a partial mobilisation on 19 May 1938. Hitler later signed a secret directive for war against Czechoslovakia to begin no later than 1 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chamberlain asked Hitler for a personal meeting and flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 15 September. At Berchtesgaden Hitler insisted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-germans">Sudeten Germans</a> be allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a> and union with Germany. On 17 September Germany began a low-intensity undeclared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and on 20 September Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> formally asked Czechoslovakia to cede the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a>. Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> made territorial demands on 21 and 22 September.</p><p>The emergency meeting of the main European powers took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 29 and 30 September. Czechoslovak representatives were in the town but were not admitted. A deal was reached on 29 September, and at about 1:30 a.m. on 30 September 1938 Hitler, Chamberlain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> and Édouard Daladier signed the agreement. The German army was to complete the occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> by 10 October, and an international commission would decide the future of other disputed areas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> it could resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> alone or submit, and on 30 September its government reluctantly capitulated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks call the agreement the Munich Diktat or the Munich Betrayal. Czechoslovakia lost its defensible border and fortifications, 70 percent of its iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry and 3.5 million citizens. On 2 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-vienna-award">First Vienna Award</a> gave southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and southern Subcarpathian Rus' to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and on 30 November Poland received small areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spis">Spiš</a> and Orava. In March 1939 the First Slovak Republic declared independence as a German puppet state, and Hitler broke his promise to respect the country's integrity by occupying the rest and creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>.</p><p>Much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> welcomed the agreement as a way of preventing a major war. Later it came to be regarded as a failed act of appeasement. Chamberlain was attacked as one of the Men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in the 1940 book Guilty Men,.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Munich Agreement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Germany remilitarized the Rhineland on 7 March 1936, nothing was done, and in 1938 Édouard Daladier signed the <b>Munich Agreement</b>, which gave the Sudetenland to Nazi Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Munich Agreement</b> of 1938 weakened Czechoslovakia, Subcarpathian Ruthenia received autonomy in October 1938, first under Andriy Brodiy and then the Greek Catholic priest Avgustyn Voloshyn.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpatho-ukraine">Carpatho-Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Munich Agreement</b> and the loss of the Sudetenland in 1938, Germany occupied the rest of Bohemia and Moravia on 15 March 1939, as Slovakia declared independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-bohemia-and-moravia">Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia</a></li></ul></section>
